{
  "architecturalBrief": {
    "planningIntent": "To create a climatically responsive, family-centric residence with a strong connection to Kerala traditional architectural values, accommodating 5 members across two floors with provisions for aging in place.",
    "siteResponse": "The East-facing plot in Kochi dictates major living areas (Verandah, Formal Living, Dining) to be oriented towards the East and North to maximize morning light and prevailing breezes while minimizing harsh West sun exposure. A central courtyard (Nadumuttam) is strategically placed to enhance passive ventilation and natural light penetration deep into the plan.",
    "orientationStrategy": "Public zones (Verandah, Formal Living) are placed towards the East for optimal morning sun and street connection. The Kitchen is placed on the North-East for cooler operations. The Ground Floor Master Bedroom is positioned on the North/West side, away from direct harsh sun, potentially opening to a shaded garden or the central courtyard for privacy and coolness. Upper floor bedrooms are strategically placed to allow cross-ventilation.",
    "publicPrivateStrategy": "A clear hierarchical zoning: Public spaces (Verandah, Foyer, Formal Living) are located at the entrance on the East. Semi-private areas (Dining, Central Courtyard, Family Lounge upstairs) form the core. Private zones (Master Bedroom on GF, secondary bedrooms on FF) are secluded in dedicated wings, ensuring privacy for family members.",
    "serviceStrategy": "Wet areas including the Kitchen, Utility, and all Bathrooms are consolidated and stacked where possible to optimize plumbing runs, reducing construction costs and improving maintenance efficiency.",
    "circulationStrategy": "A central circulation spine (Foyer to Dining, around Courtyard) integrates with a dog-legged staircase, providing clear and intuitive movement between zones and floors. The central courtyard acts as a visual and circulatory hub.",
    "climateStrategy": "Incorporation of a central 'Nadumuttam' (courtyard) for stack effect ventilation. Wide verandahs and overhangs provide shade and protection from monsoon rains. Large openings are favored on East/North, with controlled fenestration on West/South. Sloping roof (implied by style) to manage rainwater and heat.",
    "accessibilityStrategy": "The Ground Floor features a complete Master Suite with an attached bathroom, ensuring accessibility for elderly family members. The main entrance is designed for zero-step access, and internal door widths are planned for ease of movement.",
    "styleStrategy": "Embracing Kerala Traditional architecture with emphasis on sloping roofs, extensive verandahs (sit-outs), and a central courtyard (Nadumuttam). Integration of natural materials like laterite stone accents and timber details with contemporary finishes for a 'Modern Tropical' interpretation."
  },
